Description: shared/att: treat the fixed ATT CID as LE
 Linux 3.10 does not fill in l2_bdaddr_type in getsockname() for L2CAP
 sockets, so an LE ATT socket reads back as BDADDR_BREDR and io_get_type()
 misdetects the link as BR/EDR.  As a result the MTU is not fixed to the LE
 default, Exchange MTU is suppressed, and long attribute reads stop after the
 first PDU.  With HID over GATT this truncates the Report Map at 22 bytes:
 .
   hid-generic: unbalanced collection at end of report description
 .
 The fixed ATT CID (4) is LE-only, and l2_cid is reported correctly by the
 kernel, so check it first.
Author: kay <a3.works@gmail.com>
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-09-28
---
--- a/src/shared/att.c
+++ b/src/shared/att.c
@@ -1171,6 +1171,21 @@
 	if (getsockname(fd, (void *)&src, &len) < 0)
 		return -errno;
 
+	/* Some kernels (e.g. 3.10) do not fill in l2_bdaddr_type in
+	 * getsockname(), so an LE socket is indistinguishable from a BR/EDR
+	 * one by that field alone -- it reads back as BDADDR_BREDR (0).
+	 * Misdetecting the link as BR/EDR is not harmless: the Exchange MTU
+	 * sub-procedure gets suppressed (correctly, for BR/EDR) and the MTU is
+	 * taken from L2CAP_OPTIONS instead of BT_ATT_DEFAULT_LE_MTU, which
+	 * breaks long attribute reads.
+	 *
+	 * The ATT fixed CID is LE-only (BR/EDR carries ATT over PSM 31 with a
+	 * dynamically assigned CID), and the kernel does report l2_cid
+	 * correctly, so trust that first.
+	 */
+	if (src.l2_cid == htobs(BT_ATT_CID))
+		return BT_ATT_LE;
+
 	if (src.l2_bdaddr_type == BDADDR_BREDR)
 		return BT_ATT_BREDR;
 
